
CI/CD Runners Deep Dive

A Runner is the compute engine that listens for jobs, runs your pipeline steps, and reports results back to the server.

### How CI Runners Work
Ephemeral Environment: GitHub spins up a clean, isolated virtual machine (VM) specifically for your job and destroys it as soon as the job finishes.

GitHub-Hosted Runners:

Managed entirely by GitHub on clean, isolated VMs (ubuntu-latest, windows-latest, macos-latest).

Destroyed immediately after every job run (ephemeral), ensuring no leftover state between pipeline runs.

Self-Hosted Runners:

Dedicated physical machines, local servers, or private cloud VMs (AWS EC2, Azure VM) configured to run your jobs.

Use cases: Accessing private internal networks/databases without opening them to the internet, using specialized hardware (e.g., GPUs), or caching large dependencies locally to reduce build time.

Essential Pipeline Building Blocks
Now that you understand where code runs, let's explore three critical concepts used in real-world CI pipelines:

## 1. Multi-Job Workflows & Dependencies
Pipelines can have multiple jobs running either in parallel (at the same time to save time) or sequentially (in a specific order).

By default, jobs run in parallel. Use needs to make one job wait for another:

jobs:
  # Job 1: Run Unit Tests
  test:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - run: npm test

  # Job 2: Build Artifact (runs ONLY if 'test' succeeds)
  build:
    needs: test
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - run: npm run build


2. Environment Variables & Secrets Management

Real-world applications move through distinct deployment stages, each requiring different configurations, database URLs, and API credentials:

[ Feature Branch ] ──► [ Development (Dev) ] ──► [ Staging (QA / Pre-prod) ] ──► [ Production (Live) ]


name: Deploy Application

on:
  push:
    branches: [ "main" ]

jobs:
  deploy-production:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    # Links this job to the "production" environment configured in repository settings
    environment:
      name: production
      url: https://api.myapp.com

    steps:
      - name: Checkout repository code
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Deploy to Cloud
        env:
          DATABASE_URL: ${{ secrets.PROD_DB_URL }}
          API_KEY: ${{ secrets.PROD_API_KEY }}
          APP_PORT: 8080
        run: |
          echo "Deploying to production environment..."
          ./scripts/deploy.sh --db "$DATABASE_URL" --port "$APP_PORT"

Environment Variables vs. Secrets
Environment Variables (vars or plain text): Non-sensitive configuration values like port numbers (8080), log levels (DEBUG, INFO), or API base URLs ([https://api.myapp.com](https://api.myapp.com)).

Secrets (secrets): Sensitive strings like private SSH keys, cloud provider tokens, and database passwords. Stored encrypted at rest and masked with *** in build logs.


Never put API keys, database passwords, or 
deploy tokens directly in your repository or YAML files.Store them in GitHub Repository Settings 
--> Secrets and Variables --> Actions.
Reference them securely in your workflow:

steps:
      - name: Deploy to Cloud
        env:
          API_KEY: ${{ secrets.PROD_API_KEY }}
        run: |
          ./deploy.sh --key "$API_KEY"

3. Build Artifacts
Because every job runs in a separate, isolated runner, files produced in one job (like a compiled .zip or dist/ folder) do not automatically exist in the next job.

Upload Artifact: Job 1 uploads the compiled bundle.

Download Artifact: Job 2 downloads that bundle to deploy or test it.

# Inside Job 1: Save the build folder
      - name: Save build output
        uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4
        with:
          name: app-dist
          path: dist/



Quick Knowledge Check
Look at this pipeline configuration snippet:

jobs:
  lint:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - run: echo "Checking code style"

  unit-tests:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - run: echo "Running tests"

  deploy:
    needs: [lint, unit-tests]
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - run: echo "Deploying app"


Question: If the lint job passes but the unit-tests job fails, will the deploy job run?

No, the deploy job will not run.

Because deploy defines needs: [lint, unit-tests], GitHub Actions requires all prerequisite jobs in the dependency list to succeed (exit 0). If either lint or unit-tests fails, the pipeline immediately halts downstream jobs to prevent broken code from deploying.


Quick Scenario Challenge
You are configuring a deployment job for a critical production system. You want to make sure:

The deployment only proceeds after a senior engineer manually reviews and approves it.

The production database credentials cannot be accessed by feature branch runs.

Which GitHub Actions feature achieves both requirements?

A. Adding runs-on: self-hosted

B. Configuring a GitHub Environment with protection rules and environment-specific secrets

C. Hardcoding the secrets in a .env file and pushing it to Git

answer : B. Configuring a GitHub Environment with protection rules and environment-specific secrets